Chapter: VecNestedBinop
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Vector arithmetic where the operands are calls rather than let-bound
 locals, and where one vector binary operation is the operand of
 another. d0/d1/m1 are single operations over call operands; n2 and n3
 nest one operation inside another; s1 and s4 put the nested form in an
 accumulating loop, which is the shape vec-array's boxed-loop uses.

 The vector emitters allocate a LOCAL for their result, so a nested
 operation's operand pointer lives in a local rather than a temp. An
 emitter that recycles local slots around a binary operation can hand
 the output local the same register that still holds an operand
 pointer, and the operation then reads the buffer it is about to
 write. Answering exactly one operand, or zero, is the symptom.

Section: Inputs

  in-x : Integer -> Vector 2 Real
  in-x (i) = vec-splat (real-from-int i * 1.25 + 0.5)

  in-y : Integer -> Vector 2 Real
  in-y (i) = vec-splat (real-from-int i * 0.75 + 2.0)

Section: Loop

  bl : Integer, Integer, Vector 2 Real -> Vector 2 Real
  bl (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let x = in-x i
    in let y = in-y i
    in bl (i + 1) n (acc + (x * y + x / y))

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni ("d0: " & show (vec-extract (in-x 0 / in-y 0) 0))
   print-line-uni ("d1: " & show (vec-extract (in-x 1 / in-y 1) 0))
   print-line-uni ("m1: " & show (vec-extract (in-x 1 * in-y 1) 0))
   print-line-uni ("n2: " & show (vec-extract (in-x 1 * in-y 1 + in-x 0 / in-y 0) 0))
   print-line-uni ("n3: " & show (vec-extract (vec-splat 0.0 + (in-x 0 * in-y 0 + in-x 0 / in-y 0)) 0))
   print-line-uni ("s1: " & show (vec-extract (bl 0 1 (vec-splat 0.0)) 0))
   print-line-uni ("s4: " & show (vec-extract (bl 0 4 (vec-splat 0.0)) 0))
  end
